I've had plenty of games where it's been a case of:

Head across large open area, because I've finished exploring this 
batch of twisty passages without finding a means to progress, so I've got to
cross this open area.

///First off, quit exploring manually, at least for the bulk of your play.
What you gain by the manual control of where you go, you lose by losing track
of the gestalt, and by how easy it is to miss new dangers/new items. Download
the version with the Darshan patch instead, then explore using Ctrl-O and
traverse explored regions using cursor-and-go on the main map.///

Halfway across large open area, hear shout.

.oO(Crap. That's a centaur standing in that side passage that's well
within detection radius. Panic! He's faster than me and nastier than me.)

///Much. Centaurs are tied with Sigmund for #2 on my "most hated" list. #1
comes a little deeper in the dungeon, as indicated by the acronymical name of
one of my characters, "IHUH"...///

"The bolt of flame hits you!"

.oO(Ow.)

///This part may have been avoidable. There are a lot of combos with decent
stealth (lots of others with insignificant stealth too, of course), which in
many cases gives you one keypress to get out of the centaur's line of sight
if desired.

Let's assume it was unavoidable. If you're a spellcaster, you need to fight,
escape, or close (because centaurs often unwisely choose melee combat when
they're in melee range). If you need to fight, then you just keep on blasting
and quaffing; each potion of heal wounds spent is a terrible loss, to be
sure, but these are the very situations they were made for. If you need to
escape, it's a question of just teleporting, or scroll-blinking and then
teleporting. Scroll-blinking is a terrible cost, but when the centaur stands
a good chance of killing you in the turn it takes to read the ?oTel, there's
not much else to be done (unless this is because you are wounded and there's
a good chance that a !oHW will remedy this). Unless you're VERY sure you can
cut LOS by rounding a corner, choosing not to scroll-blinking tends to have a
deep cost in terms of healing, but again, scrolls of blinking are rarer and
more powerful than !oHW, so it's a tough call.

The thing with Crawl is that although alternative approaches to ID are
extremely limited (shops are rare, and you can only identify bad things
by buying them, not by selling them, because you can't sell things),
potions (degeneration, slowing, poison, strong poison), scrolls
(immolation, torment), and wands whose use (hasting, healing, ...) by
you can lead directly to your death still exist.

You can't even have any faith in statistically-guided
identification-by-use; I've found my stack of five identical potions to
be slowing or confusion or poison or degeneration far too many times,
and because you can't see what kind of potion/scroll/wand the monsters
are using (only that they're using something of that type), you can't
even get the monsters to identify things for you.
